# A folded address that is holding scratch registers of its own.
#
# An `x86_addr` produces no register and emits no bytes: it is resolved at its own position, one
# instruction in front of the access that reads it, and its base and its index are live *into* that
# access in whatever locations they were resolved to. When either came out of the frame, that
# location is a scratch register - the operand pool is taken from the top of the register file, and
# so is the reload the address needed.
#
# So the access cannot hand those same registers to its own operands, and `takeTemp` steps past every
# position the address is still holding. What is pinned below is the arithmetic of that step: the
# address takes r15 and r14, and the temporary the access wants is r13 rather than either of them. A
# pool that stopped counting the skipped positions would put the value being loaded, or stored, into
# the register the address is being read through.
#
# The depth this reaches is a property of the form table rather than of these two functions -
# `operandTempReach` in machine.cpp is what checks it, for every form, against the pool's width.
#
# The Clobber convention destroys every register except rbp, so a value that has to survive one of
# its calls can only be in rbp and everything after the first is in the frame. That is the smallest
# way to spill something deliberately - see MemoryOperand.lower, which uses it for the same reason.

sink<clobber>(%x: Int): Int {
    { ret %x }
}

# Two addresses over four values, so at most one of the four keeps a register: the second address has
# both halves in the frame and takes two scratch registers for them. The value it loads has to
# survive a second call, so its home is a slot as well - which is what makes the access want a
# temporary at all, and it is the one that has to come from below the address.
scratchAddress(%arg: Int, %p: Ptr, %i: Long, %p2: Ptr, %i2: Long): Int {
    {
        %r = call_clobber sink, %arg: Int
        %q = add %p, %i: Ptr
        %v = load %q, 4: Int
        %q2 = add %p2, %i2: Ptr
        %v2 = load %q2, 4: Int
        %s = call_clobber sink, %r: Int
        %t = add %v, %v2: Int
        %u = add %t, %s: Int
        ret %u
    }
}

# The same skip on the other side of the access: what needs the temporary here is the value being
# stored rather than a result being produced, and a store has no memory-to-memory form to fall back
# on. %tail is what takes rbp, which is what leaves the stored value in the frame.
scratchAddressStore(%arg: Int, %keep: Int, %p: Ptr, %i: Long, %tail: Int): Int {
    {
        %r = call_clobber sink, %arg: Int
        %q = add %p, %i: Ptr
        store %q, %keep, 4
        %s = add %tail, %r: Int
        %u = add %s, %tail: Int
        ret %u
    }
}
